 },
 {
  "id": "S116",
  "article_id": "gnews:4249e0578b11cedf5a71ff08",
  "title": "Maximize Spectral Efficiency with AI-Native RAN and NVIDIA AI Aerial",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-07",
  "article_opening": "By Cindy Goh\nNVIDIA AI Aerial enables an algorithms-first approach that shifts RAN design from fitting CPU compute budgets to maximizing spectral efficiency through AI-native Layer 1 and Layer 2 models.\nGPU acceleration unlocks higher-order spatial multiplexing, with ML-based beamforming delivering up to 1.62x throughput gains over traditional zero-forcing methods in 64T64R massive MIMO scenarios.\nDeep reinforcement learning link adaptation achieves 1.3x throughput improvement over outer-loop li",
  "passage": "By Cindy Goh\nNVIDIA AI Aerial enables an algorithms-first approach that shifts RAN design from fitting CPU compute budgets to maximizing spectral efficiency through AI-native Layer 1 and Layer 2 models.\nGPU acceleration unlocks higher-order spatial multiplexing, with ML-based beamforming delivering up to 1.62x throughput gains over traditional zero-forcing methods in 64T64R massive MIMO scenarios.\nDeep reinforcement learning link adaptation achieves 1.3x throughput improvement over outer-loop link adaptation at the cell edge while maintaining latency below the 30-microsecond RAN budget.\nAcademic research validates these gains, including a 20% spectral efficiency improvement from pilotless spatial multiplexing and up to 70% throughput increase for practical DRL link adaptation in high-mobility conditions.\nThe platform supports integrated sensing and communications workloads and enables operators to monetize off-peak GPU capacity by hosting edge AI inference applications.\nNVIDIA is collaborating with Nokia to deliver AI-RAN platforms integrated with No…",
  "statement": "ML-based beamforming delivering up to 1.62x throughput gains over traditional zero-forcing methods"
 },
 {
  "id": "S117",
  "article_id": "gnews:f0d529209a91c7a237e117f1",
  "title": "Nvidia chips case: Man charged with fraud allegedly used over $38m in ill-gotten gains to buy house",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-06",
  "article_opening": "Nvidia chips case: Man charged with fraud allegedly used over $38m in ill-gotten gains to buy house\nOn July 6, Alan Wei Zhaolun was handed nine additional charges – three counts of money laundering and six counts of fraud.\nAlan Wei Zhaolun faces 11 charges including fraud and money laundering.\nWei allegedly conspired with Aaron Woon and Jenny Lim to commit fraud.\nThree companies linked to him - A-Speed Infotech, Aperia International and Aperia Cloud Services (II) - were also charged with fraud o",
  "passage": "…the trio engaged in a conspiracy to commit fraud linked to the purchase of servers from three suppliers – Dell, Super Micro Computer and Asus.\nWithout revealing further details, a police spokesperson added: “The charges asserted that... they falsely represented, in communications to these suppliers, that one of the Aperia Group companies would be the end-user of the servers.”\nOn July 6, Aperia International was charged with five counts of fraud, while Aperia Cloud Services (II) was handed two such charges.\nA-Speed Infotech was charged with one count of fraud.\nThe pre-trial conferences for the three companies and Wei will be held on Aug 14.\nWei is represented by lawyers from WongPartnership.\nIn a statement on July 6, they said their client and the companies will be contesting all the charges against them.\nThey added: “The companies and Mr Wei are fully cooperating with the authorities so that the regulatory flux is taken into consideration when reviewing the alleged offences.\n“The charges relate to commercial transactions involving suppliers and customers from the (United States) and elsewhere, which required multi-jurisdictional regulatory compliance.”\nWei, Woon and a third man, Li Ming, 52, were first hauled to court in February 2025.\nThis was after Singapore came under the spotlight in a US investigation into whether Chinese start-up DeepSeek had circumvented US restrictions on advanced Nvidia chips by buying them from third parties in other countries, including the Republic.\nThe three me…",
  "statement": "In a statement on July 6, they said their client and the companies will be contesting all the charges against them."
 },
 {
  "id": "S118",
  "article_id": "gnews:f8f440b5020fec9773812b6e",
  "title": "Best Artificial Intelligence (AI) Stock: AMD, Nvidia, or Broadcom",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-12",
  "article_opening": "If you asked me at the start of the year how I would rank Advanced Micro Devices ( AMD +3.12% ) , Broadcom ( AVGO +2.89% ) , and Nvidia ( NVDA +2.61% ) in terms of potential stock performance, I would have put Nvidia at the top, Broadcom in the middle, and AMD in last place. So far in 2026, my projection that Broadcom would be in the middle has panned out, but AMD has been a massive winner while Nvidia has been a loser.\nThere's still a lot of time left in 2026, but which one of these three artif",
  "passage": "…roadcom as a tie.\nNvidia is still dominating the growth game\nFrom a growth standpoint, Nvidia still stands alone at the top, growing far faster than the other two.\nLooking ahead, the market still expects Nvidia to maintain its lead for the full fiscal year, though Broadcom is starting to close the gap.\nAMD is still well behind Nvidia, and while I think Broadcom is still a strong pick, Nvidia will likely retain its rapid-growth title for some time.\nLastly, let's look at valuation. Because all three of these stocks are rapidly growing, the best way to value them is by using earnings projections. For the current fiscal year, AMD's stock is far more expensive than either Broadcom's or Nvidia's. Furthermore, Broadcom is also more than 50% more expensive than Nvidia's.\nIf we use next fiscal year's projections, the trend remains the same: Nvidia's stock looks dirt cheap compared to the other two.\nClearly, Nvidia is the winner here as well.\nInvestors should sell AMD shares for Nvidia shares\nI think the biggest takeaway here is that AMD's 2026 rally has likely gotten ahead of itself, and investors are discounting Nvidia far too much, as its display is being far more successful than AMD's. Broadcom is still a solid stock pick with strong growth head, but it just isn't as good a deal as Nvidia right now.\nBy Keithen Drury – Jul 12, 2026 at 6:05PM EST",
  "statement": "Furthermore, Broadcom is also more than 50% more expensive than Nvidia's."
 },
 {
  "id": "S119",
  "article_id": "gnews:88d4eb777f28ef9f6f8e2426",
  "title": "NVIDIA Launches Open “Ising” Decoder Architecture to Suppress Quantum Color Code Error Rates by 347x",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-13",
  "article_opening": "NVIDIA’s Quantum Computing Division has introduced Ising , an open-source model family designed to deploy neural-network-driven control layers for fault-tolerant quantum error correction (QEC). Detailed in a technical disclosure ( “ NVIDIA Ising Decoding Cuts Color Code Logical Error Rates by Over 300X “ ), the launch features the Ising Decoder ColorCode 1 Fast, a 17-layer 3D Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) engineered to function as a localized pre-decoder for triangular color codes.\nIn bench",
  "passage": "…d Software Integration\nThe platform interfaces with existing high-performance computing (HPC) environments through a data pipeline built on the NVIDIA cuStabilizer library , which is integrated into the broader cuQuantum simulation stack. During training, the cuStabilizer backend generates synthetic fault logs modeling circuit-level noise (such as the standard si1000 noise model family). PyTorch then optimizes the 2.9-million-parameter network weights to match the specific noise characteristics of the underlying Quantum Processing Unit (QPU).\nUsers can select deeper or shallower model layers to navigate a customized trade-off between baseline decoder accuracy and round-trip latency budgets. The entire framework has been open-sourced under an Apache 2.0 license, providing public access to model weights, training scripts, and architectural blueprints. This allows quantum hardware manufacturers to modify the neural layers to align with their specific physical noise distributions, creating custom decoding pipelines that match their hardware limits.\nReview the complete open-source codebases, deployment cookbooks, and parameter weights on the NVIDIA Ising Architecture .",
  "statement": "The entire framework has been open-sourced under an Apache 2.0 license"
 },
 {
  "id": "S120",
  "article_id": "gnews:3345f0d91321165a099860c9",
  "title": "Singapore Seizes $42M Mansion in Nvidia Chip Smuggling Probe｜Daily News Digest｜2026-07-02｜web only",
  "publication_day": "2026-07-02",
  "article_opening": "Singapore Seizes $42M Mansion in Nvidia Chip Smuggling Probe\nJuly 2, 2026 -- Today’s top stories: Singapore Seizes $42M Mansion in Nvidia Chip Smuggling Probe, Rubio Would Step Aside for Vance in 2028 White House Race, and Google Ordered To Pay Klarna $1.97b in Antitrust Damages.\nBy CommonWealth Magazine\nSingapore seizes $42m home in Nvidia chip smuggling case\nSingapore authorities have seized a multimillion-dollar mansion and charged people with money laundering as part of a case of suspected c",
